('128.6.b.5',2,'A student reads a news article, discusses it and writes a response. The student used:','["only reading", "no skills", "all four language skills", "only writing"]'::jsonb,2,'Listening, speaking, reading and writing combine.','original'),
('128.6.b.5',2,'Which activity combines listening and writing?','["reading alone", "silent drawing", "singing", "taking notes during a talk"]'::jsonb,3,'Notetaking links listening and writing.','original'),
('128.6.b.6.A',2,'Setting a purpose before reading means:','["deciding why you are reading", "choosing a pencil", "counting the pages", "finishing quickly"]'::jsonb,0,'A purpose guides reading.','original'),
('128.6.b.6.A',2,'A purpose for reading an assigned science chapter might be:','["to count paragraphs", "to answer questions about the water cycle", "to find a fun font", "to avoid vocabulary"]'::jsonb,1,'Purposes connect to learning goals.','original'),
('128.6.b.6.B',3,'Which is a question to ask during reading?','["Who has the biggest desk?", "What is for dinner?", "What does this word mean in this sentence?", "Where did I put my shoes?"]'::jsonb,2,'Questions during reading monitor understanding.','original'),
('128.6.b.6.B',3,'Generating questions after reading helps you to:','["forget the text", "avoid rereading", "skip the ending", "check and deepen understanding"]'::jsonb,3,'Questions extend thinking.','original'),
('128.6.b.6.C',3,'Characteristics of genre can help you predict because:','["texts in a genre follow similar patterns", "genres have no patterns", "genres hide the plot", "all genres are identical"]'::jsonb,0,'Genre patterns suggest what comes next.','original'),
('128.6.b.6.C',3,'Reading a fairy tale, you predict a happy ending. You used:','["the book''s weight", "characteristics of the genre", "the author''s age", "the page count"]'::jsonb,1,'Fairy tales often end happily.','original'),
('128.6.b.6.D',3,'A mental image of a stormy night in a story helps you to:','["skip the scene", "forget the setting", "feel the mood of the scene", "change the plot"]'::jsonb,2,'Imagery deepens understanding.','original'),
('128.6.b.6.D',3,'Which sentence helps you picture a scene best?','["It was there.", "Something happened.", "The road was.", "Golden light spilled across the quiet, dusty road."]'::jsonb,3,'Sensory details create images.','original'),
('128.6.b.6.E',3,'A text-to-self connection links a text to:','["your own experience", "the news", "a map", "another book"]'::jsonb,0,'Self connections come from your life.','original'),
('128.6.b.6.E',3,'A character''s courage makes you think of a historical hero you studied. That is a:','["text-to-self connection", "text-to-world connection", "glossary entry", "spelling pattern"]'::jsonb,1,'Connections to society and history are text-to-world.','original'),
('128.6.b.6.F',3,'A character yawns and rubs his eyes all through class. A good inference is that he:','["just finished lunch", "won a prize", "did not sleep well", "is cheerful"]'::jsonb,2,'Clues support the inference of tiredness.','original'),
('128.6.b.6.F',3,'To support an inference, a reader should cite:','["a rumor", "a guess", "the cover", "evidence from the text"]'::jsonb,3,'Inferences need text evidence.','original'),
